=== ADHD Comorbidity: Screening and Treatment Questions
<subsec:adhd-comorbidity-screening>

Two facts frame this subsection. ADHD is the one comorbidity here with a validated screening instrument, so a *candidate* high-risk subgroup can be flagged without a specialist referral. And there is *no mortality evidence in ME/CFS* for ADHD or its treatment: every mortality figure in this paper comes from general-population ADHD cohorts. ADHD in ME/CFS should therefore be treated for function, attention and quality of life — never for a survival benefit that has not been shown.

#speculation(title: [ADHD Screening as Mortality-Risk Stratification in ME/CFS])[
*Certainty: 0.20.* Persistent adult ADHD was present in 20.9% of a CFS cohort (n = 158, single tertiary sample), and the comorbid group showed higher suicide-risk markers @SaezFrancas2012adhdcfs. In general-population ADHD cohorts the mortality excess concentrates in suicide and unintentional injury @Corrigan2026ADHDmortality @Dalsgaard2015ADHDmortality. A validated screen already used in this paper's neurodivergent-risk work — the WURS-25, 25 items, usual threshold above 36 — could be applied at intake. Note the construct gap: the WURS-25 scores *retrospective childhood* ADHD symptoms, whereas the ME/CFS suicide-risk finding concerns *persistent adult* ADHD (20.9% versus 29.7% childhood). A positive childhood screen is therefore a proxy, and adult persistence needs clinical confirmation.

The proposal is a *stratification* step, not a diagnosis and not a treatment: a positive screen would flag patients for confirmatory specialist assessment and a suicide-risk review. Whether the screen adds anything beyond the depression screening a clinic already performs is unproven, and no study shows that screening changes mortality. Until the independent-effect question is settled (see @oq:adhd-impulsivity-channel), screening should sit inside existing suicide-risk assessment rather than becoming a separate pathway. This extends, rather than replaces, the WURS-25 risk-enrichment proposal in @oq:wurs-risk-flag.

Two practical limits apply. The screen is a 25-item cognitive-recall task, which very-severe and bedbound patients may not tolerate — for that group it should be caregiver-administered or deferred. And its sensitivity and specificity in a population where cognitive complaints are near-universal are unknown.

(Evidence type: general-population observational plus one cross-sectional ME/CFS cohort. Severity applicability: unknown — no severity-stratified data; screening yield is untested in severe and very-severe cohorts. Not yet replicated.)

*Falsifiable prediction:* in an ambulatory ME/CFS clinic, screen-positive patients show a higher rate of suicide-risk markers than screen-negative patients, *and* the screen adds predictive information over depression screening alone (pre-specified increment in discrimination, not a yield threshold). The stratification claim is refuted if ADHD screening adds nothing once depression screening is included in the model.

*Consequence:* a short questionnaire could make a hidden suicide-risk subgroup visible at intake — cheap, but unproven as a way to prevent deaths, and it does not replace depression screening.
] <spec:adhd-screening-stratification>

#open-question(title: [Do Non-Stimulant ADHD Medications Offer a Safer Route in ME/CFS?])[
Guanfacine (an $alpha$2A agonist) and atomoxetine (a selective noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor) treat ADHD without the dopamine-surge arousal that makes stimulants risky in ME/CFS. On mechanism alone they are an attractive way to treat the comorbidity without importing the stimulant harm profile. Three problems block a recommendation.

First, there are no ME/CFS data for either drug — not a trial, not a case series in this paper's evidence base. Second, both interact with common ME/CFS co-prescriptions, in opposite directions. Guanfacine *lowers* blood pressure and heart rate: it opposes the pressor action of midodrine and fludrocortisone (risking worse orthostatic intolerance) and compounds beta-blocker bradycardia. Atomoxetine *raises* heart rate and blood pressure and can worsen orthostatic tachycardia; it also causes insomnia and appetite suppression, both unhelpful in ME/CFS. Third, the relevant outcome is uncertain: the ADHD mortality association is driven by suicide and injury, and there is no evidence that either drug changes those outcomes @Corrigan2026ADHDmortality @Farhat2025CVsafety @Hennissen2017CVmeta.

If either drug were tried, the minimum safe protocol would be supine and standing blood pressure and heart rate at baseline, at one week, at four weeks, and after every dose change, stopping or reducing for presyncope, syncope or new bradycardia. No validated ME/CFS monitoring protocol exists; this is general clinical monitoring applied to the known pharmacology.

*Discriminating measurement:* a small crossover trial in ME/CFS with comorbid ADHD testing executive function (not fatigue) and PEM frequency versus placebo, with orthostatic vitals as a pre-specified safety endpoint. (Severity applicability: unknown — no data in any severity stratum.)

*Consequence:* a non-stimulant option would let clinicians treat ADHD in ME/CFS without the crash risk, but until it is tested in this population the idea remains a hypothesis with a plausible mechanism and no safety data. Nothing here justifies treating ADHD to prolong life.
] <oq:adhd-nonstimulant-option>
